# Descriptor for this agent. agent-mgr supplies everything else.
#
# Every key here is optional -- agent-mgr derives all of them from the agent's
# name. Set one only to override the convention, and say why.
#
#   AGENT_HOME       default ~/.hermes-<name>
#   AGENT_CONTAINER  default hermes-<name>
#   AGENT_PROJECT    default hermes-<name>
#   AGENT_TZ         default America/Los_Angeles -- fleet-wide only. This
#                    file is read by EVERY instance registered against
#                    this repo, so a zone belonging to one person goes in
#                    that instance's own $AGENT_HOME/.env instead -- which is
#                    ~/.hermes-<name> by convention, but follows a declared
#                    AGENT_HOME if this file sets one.
#   AGENT_IMAGE      default the hermes_local image in runtime/stack.json
#   AGENT_CONFIG     default config.yaml, resolved against this directory
#   AGENT_LIVE       default 0. Set 1 when real people's workflows run
#                    through this agent -- the gateway messages its person at
#                    every restart, so a restart is user-visible. agent-mgr
#                    then asks [y/N] at a terminal before any transition, and
#                    refuses non-interactively unless AGENT_TRANSITION_ACK=1
#                    is set -- the explicit acknowledgement for automation
#                    that means to restart.
#
# Three more, optional and unset by default. All are paths in this repo,
# resolved against this directory; the two hooks must be executable:
#
#   AGENT_DEPLOY_HOOK       a script `agent-mgr deploy` runs after its own
#                           installs -- this agent's own deploy step, such as
#                           seeding a corpus. Sequenced by agent-mgr so that one
#                           command is the whole deploy -- except crons, which
#                           are `cron-sync`'s. A non-zero exit fails the deploy.
#
#   AGENT_PRE_TRANSITION    this agent's veto on stopping or replacing its
#                           container. Every route to a transition goes through
#                           one seam, so it is asked before all of them -- the
#                           reload that follows a write included -- and a
#                           non-zero exit refuses. Use it for work that must not
#                           be interrupted; the rentals agent checks for an
#                           in-flight ingest.
#
#                           Two things to know when writing one. `activate` is
#                           the one command a refusal does NOT fail: it has
#                           already spent a one-time activation by then, so it
#                           reports success and skips the restart rather than
#                           inviting you to spend another. And `deploy` asks
#                           twice -- once as a preflight before it writes
#                           anything, again at the reload it ends with -- so
#                           write the guard to be safe to ask more than once.
#
#   AGENT_CRON_SPEC         a JSON file of this agent's shipped scheduled jobs,
#                           which `agent-mgr cron-sync` converges onto the
#                           running gateway's scheduler. Data, not a program.
#                           Unset means this agent ships no jobs, and cron-sync
#                           refuses to run rather than silently no-op.
#
# Both hooks receive this descriptor's non-AGENT_ variables in their environment.
#
# An instance that needs a derived image or extra mounts adds a
# compose.override.yml beside this file and sets the variables it names here.
